#N canvas 40 60 1280 1360 12;
#X declare -path ..;
#X obj 0 0 cnv 1 1280 46 empty empty 17\ \ \ Reinforcement\ learning\ \ -\ \ a\ melody\ learns\ from\ reward\ (Q-learning\,\ Watkins\ 1989) 14 23 0 20 #1d3557 #ffffff 0;
#X obj 1110 1330 declare -path ..;
#X text 800 1330 finds the nn.* objects one folder up ->, f 42;
#X text 20 1330 neuralplay - Moisés Horta Valenzuela / SEMILLA.AI Studio, f 70;
#X text 20 56 No teacher tells this network the right notes. It plays \, and only when a phrase lands on the goal note does it get a reward. Q-learning turns those rare rewards into a sense of direction: after a few dozen phrases every melody finds its way home. The same idea learned backgammon \, Atari and Go., f 175;
#X obj 10 96 cnv 1 390 810 empty empty THE\ IDEA:\ learning\ from\ reward 8 12 0 13 #eef1f5 #1d3557 0;
#X text 20 122 In lessons 4 to 6 a teacher gave the network the right answer for every example. REINFORCEMENT LEARNING gets no answers \, only a score - a REWARD - and often only at the end: a dog learning a trick \, a child learning to walk \, an improviser who feels when a phrase has landed., f 52;
#X text 20 218 THE LOOP: the AGENT sees a STATE (the note it is on) and chooses an ACTION (a move: 2 down \, 1 down \, 1 up \, 2 up). The WORLD answers with the next state and a reward: +1 for landing on the goal note (the phrase ends) \, 0 for anything else., f 52;
#X text 20 314 Q-LEARNING keeps a table Q(note \, move) = the reward it expects if it makes that move now and plays its best afterwards. After every move it corrects one number:, f 52;
#X text 30 380 Q <- Q + alpha * (r + gamma * maxQ(next) - Q), f 52;
#X text 20 401 The bracket is the SURPRISE: what it got \, plus what it now expects from the next note \, minus what it expected before., f 52;
#X text 20 452 CREDIT TRAVELS BACK: only the last note is rewarded \, but each note inherits gamma times the value of its best next note. In the value graph the notes next to the goal learn first \, then the value spreads outwards \, phrase after phrase., f 52;
#X text 20 548 EXPLORE OR EXPLOIT: with probability epsilon the agent tries a random move (you hear a dull pluck) \, otherwise it plays its best (a clear bell). Never exploring \, it keeps the first way home it found. Always exploring \, it never uses what it knows., f 52;
#X text 20 644 IT IS A NETWORK: the note playing is a one-hot input \, the 4 moves are 4 output neurons \, Q is the weights - and the update is the rule of lesson 4 \, w = w + rate * (t - y) * x \, with a target t the network makes itself. DQN (2013) put a deep network there and learned Atari games from the pixels., f 52;
#X text 20 755 IN MUSIC: RL Tuner (Magenta \, 2017) rewarded a melody network for following music theory \, much like the goal here \, and MusicRL (2024) tuned a music generator with the ratings of thousands of listeners. In examples/rl-applause-drummer.pd YOU are the listener., f 52;
#X obj 10 1116 cnv 1 1260 210 empty empty A\ SHORT\ HISTORY\ OF\ LEARNING\ FROM\ REWARD 8 12 0 13 #f4ecfa #1d3557 0;
#X text 30 1144 1898 Thorndike puts cats in puzzle boxes: what brings satisfaction is "stamped in" (the law of effect)., f 86;
#X text 30 1188 1951 Minsky builds SNARC: 40 neurons of vacuum tubes and motors learn a maze from reward., f 86;
#X text 30 1232 1961 Michie's MENACE: 304 matchboxes of beads learn noughts and crosses (a win adds beads)., f 86;
#X text 30 1276 1983 Barto \, Sutton & Anderson: an actor neuron and a critic neuron learn to balance a pole., f 86;
#X text 660 1144 1988-1992 temporal differences (Sutton) \, Q-learning (Watkins) \, REINFORCE (Williams) \, TD-Gammon (Tesauro) learns backgammon by playing itself., f 86;
#X text 660 1188 2013-2016 DQN learns Atari from pixels \, AlphaGo beats Lee Sedol at Go., f 86;
#X text 660 1232 2017-2022 RL from human feedback: people rate the answers of a network \, RL makes the good ones more likely (ChatGPT)., f 86;
#X text 660 1276 2024 Barto and Sutton receive the Turing award (announced in 2025)., f 86;
