#X text 870 866 the pictures \, the buttons and the test hooks, f 56;
#X obj 10 916 cnv 1 1260 190 empty empty TRY\ THIS 8 12 0 13 #e6f6e9 #1d3557 0;
#X text 30 942 1) Press play and raise the volume. At first the melody wanders. Watch the value graph: the notes next to the goal light up first \, then the light spreads outwards. After 30 to 50 phrases nearly every phrase runs straight home., f 56;
#X text 30 1023 2) Gamma 0: only the very next reward counts - it learns the last move and wanders everywhere else. Gamma 1: every note is worth 1 \, it knows it will get home but no longer hurries., f 56;
#X text 446 942 3) Epsilon 0: it repeats the first way home it found (fast \, stubborn \, rarely the shortest). Epsilon 1: pure dice \, it never uses what it knows., f 56;
#X text 446 993 4) Click another GOAL: the old knowledge points the wrong way \, and you hear it unlearn. Press practice to skip 200 phrases in an instant., f 56;
#X text 862 942 5) Be the critic: press good! right after a move you like - try rewarding leaps. Each press adds alpha to that one number \, like the human ratings of RLHF. Reward a detour often enough and it learns the detour: it does what you reward \, not what you meant., f 56;
#X text 862 1038 6) Next: examples/rl-applause-drummer.pd \, a drummer that learns from your claps - a policy of firing neurons instead of a table., f 56;
#N canvas 40 60 900 300 defaults 0;
